---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About the Project"
  description="Why we treat dishes like systems to be diagrammed rather than photorealistic gimmicks."
>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16">
    <span class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-blueprint-accent bg-blueprint-bg px-3 py-1 rounded border border-blueprint-accent/30 inline-block mb-4">
      System Architecture
    </span>
    <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-serif font-bold text-warm-950 mb-6 leading-tight">
      Why "Engineered Food"?
    </h1>

    <div class="space-y-6 text-warm-800 font-sans text-lg leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        Traditional recipe blogs rely heavily on stylized food photography. While appealing, photos don't explain <strong>structural hierarchy</strong>, <strong>layering logic</strong>, or <strong>component interactions</strong>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Realistic 3D food renders often suffer from the "uncanny valley" of food—waxy textures that look unappetizing. At <strong>Engineered Food</strong>, we deliberately embrace low-poly, diagrammatic 3D models and exploded views.
      </p>

      <div class="my-8 p-6 bg-blueprint-bg text-blueprint-text rounded-2xl border border-blueprint-accent/30 font-mono text-sm leading-relaxed shadow-lg">
        <p class="text-blueprint-accent font-bold mb-2">// Core Philosophy</p>
        <p>"Dishes are assemblies. By treating recipes like blueprinted systems, home cooks gain instant clarity on component stacking, moisture barriers, heat transfer, and structural integrity."</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-serif font-bold text-warm-950 pt-4">Distribution & Design</h2>
      <p>
        Our format is optimized specifically for Pinterest and visual search: vertical high-contrast stills, short looping 3D assembly animations, and schema-compliant Recipe Rich Pins.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
